g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/97891482?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH","html_url":"https://github.com/EdwardChanCH","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-03-09T18:19:02Z","updated_at":"2026-03-09T19:09:32Z","body":"Now that you mentioned it, the Godot Doc implies that `@GlobalScope.min()` will work on `Vector` types, when in fact it only accepts `int` and `float` types:\r\nhttps://docs.godotengine.org/en/4.6/classes/class_%40globalscope.html#class-globalscope-method-min\r\n\r\nEdit: I updated the docs and code to also accept `Vector` types, so that it becomes consistent with `Array.min()`. The first commit is kept for comparison.","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/4025825563/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/4030619654","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/117240#issuecomment-4030619654","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/117240","id":4030619654,"node_id":"IC_kwDOAO6SJc7wPmAG","user":{"login":"AThousandShips","id":96648715,"node_id":"U_kgDOBcK-Cw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/96648715?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips","html_url":"https://github.com/AThousandShips","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-03-10T11:13:40Z","updated_at":"2026-03-10T11:13:40Z","body":"I don't think adding `VectorX` to this, it'll produce a lot of unexpected results, as per the note, instead for that case I'd say the note should be made clearer instead","author_association":"MEMBER","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/4030619654/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/4032242086","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/117240#issuecomment-4032242086","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/117240","id":4032242086,"node_id":"IC_kwDOAO6SJc7wVyGm","user":{"login":"dalexeev","id":47700418,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ3NzAwNDE4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/47700418?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dalexeev","html_url":"https://github.com/dalexeev","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dalexeev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dalexeev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dalexeev/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dalexeev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dalexeev/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dalexeev/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dalexeev/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dalexeev/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dalexeev/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-03-10T15:21:16Z","updated_at":"2026-03-10T15:21:16Z","body":"In my opinion, we need to answer the following questions before we can move forward:\r\n\r\n1. How should the `min()` and `max()` functions behave when passed arguments of different types?\r\na. Report an error. That is, the second and subsequent arguments should have the same type as the first.\r\nb. Perform \"promotion\". That is, `max(false, true) == true`, `max(0, true) == 1`, `max(0, 1.0) == 1.0`.\r\n2. Should the `min()` and `max()` functions support only scalar numeric types? Vector types? All types with the `<` and `>` operators are defined?\r\n\r\nOnce these questions are answered, we can update the implementation and documentation, add test cases.","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/4032242086/reactions","total_count":3,"+1":3,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/4289943855","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/117240#issuecomment-4289943855","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/117240","id":4289943855,"node_id":"IC_kwDOAO6SJc7_s1kv","user":{"login":"EdwardChanCH","id":97891482,"node_id":"U_kgDOBdW0mg","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/97891482?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH","html_url":"https://github.com/EdwardChanCH","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-04-21T15:52:15Z","updated_at":"2026-04-21T15:53:25Z","body":"> 1. How should the `min()` and `max()` functions behave when passed arguments of different types?\r\n> a. Report an error. That is, the second and subsequent arguments should have the same type as the first.\r\n> b. Perform \"promotion\". That is, `max(false, true) == true`, `max(0, true) == 1`, `max(0, 1.0) == 1.0`.\r\n\r\nI think (1a) is more consistent with how `Array.min()`/`Array.max()` works:\r\n```gdscript\r\nfunc _ready() -> void:\r\n\tvar a: Array = [1, 1.0, true]\r\n\tprint(a.min()) # Returns <null>.\r\n```\r\n\r\nI think (1b) could be confusing, since `@GlobalScope.min()`/`@GlobalScope.max()` have untyped parameters. If the user mixes types (except `int/float`) then it should return an error, because it is equally valid to cast `int/float` --> `bool`, or `bool` --> `int/float`:\r\n\r\n```gdscript\r\nfunc test_bool(b: bool) -> bool:\r\n\treturn b\r\n\r\nfunc test_int(i: int) -> int:\r\n\treturn i\r\n\r\nfunc _ready() -> void:\r\n\tprint(test_bool(1)) # Returns true.\r\n\tprint(test_int(true)) # Returns 1.\r\n\tprint(min(1, true)) # Ambiguous. Returns true or 1?\r\n```\r\n\r\n> 2. Should the min() and max() functions support only scalar numeric types? Vector types? All types with the < and > operators are defined?\r\n\r\nI think the last option is more consistent with how `Array.min()`/`Array.max()` works.\r\n\r\nOf all the `Variant` types:\r\n| `<`,`>` defined | `min()`,`max()` defined |\r\n| ------------- | ------------- |\r\n| Array  | Array  |\r\n| VectorN | VectorN |\r\n| int |  |\r\n| float |  |\r\n| bool |  |\r\n| String |  |\r\n| StringName |  |\r\n| RID |  |\r\n\r\n...most types do not have their own `min()` `max()` methods, so I would argue that `Array.min()` `VectorN.min()` are the exceptions rather than the norm. Thus, `@GlobalScope.min()` should work on any type with defined `<` and `>` operators, instead of defined `min()` and `max()` methods.\r\n\r\nWe could add a note like: \"To find the component-wise minimum, see `Array.min()` and `Vector.min()`\".\r\n\r\n```gdscript\r\nfunc _ready() -> void:\r\n\tvar a: Array = [0, false, 0.0]\r\n\tvar b: Array = [0, false, 1.0]\r\n\tprint([a, b].min()) # Returns [0, false, 0.0]\r\n\t\r\n\tvar c: Vector3 = Vector3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0)\r\n\tvar d: Vector3 = Vector3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0)\r\n\tprint([c, d].min()) # Returns (0.0, 0.0, 1.0)\r\n```","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/4289943855/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/4750422063","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/117240#issuecomment-4750422063","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/117240","id":4750422063,"node_id":"IC_kwDOAO6SJc8AAAABGyWwLw","user":{"login":"EdwardChanCH","id":97891482,"node_id":"U_kgDOBdW0mg","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/97891482?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH","html_url":"https://github.com/EdwardChanCH","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/EdwardChanCH/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-06-19T09:44:47Z","updated_at":"2026-06-19T09:44:47Z","body":"Would it be better if I split this PR? This original PR for allowing `bool` (mostly in agreement) and a new PR for adding all other `Variant` types (still in discussion)?","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/4750422063/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null}]